Testing code snippet:
```csharp
[Test, TestCaseSource("StateTest"), Category("ArrayList")]
[ExpectedException(typeof(IndexOutOfRangeException), "Should throw if the specified index is not valid.")]
public void Test_MyFunc([Values(0, 1, 10, -1)] int index, bool assertException)
{
    ArrayList list = new ArrayList(new object[] {1, 2, 3, 4, 5});

    var exception = Record.Exception(() => MyFunc(list, index, assertException));

    if (assertException)
    {
        Assert.IsNotNull(exception);
    }
    else
    {
        Assert.IsNull(exception);
    }
}
```
This testing code snippet is calling a method MyFunc with values source from enum value 0,1,10 and -1. It also tests a situation when exception should be thrown if the specified index is not valid.

PexSettings:
```csharp
[assembly: PexAssemblySettings(
  MaxConstraintSolverTime = "1.00:00:00",
  SearchTimeout               = "7:00:00")]

```
```
These settings are increased to have enough time to find new test cases and to have smaller search timeout to have early timeouts of the test run for more interesting paths of test generation. Especially, consider to increase the timeout according to the size of your data and your machine capacity.
```
Testing of any code involves various methods as explained in this blog post. The code contracts, code coverage, mutation testing, statistical testing, and fault testing are some of the methods used. These are all part of the Software testing lifecycle, and all are valuable tools for improving the accuracy and efficiency of the software testing process.
```
```
In this article, we used the NUnit assertion framework for testing the Add and Remove functions of an ArrayList class. We started by setting up a simple test method for each function, which created an ArrayList instance and called the functions. For our tests, we created test cases where we passed an ArrayList through all the functions and checked the output. This was a simple yet effective way to test those methods.
```
```
These tests were performed successfully. They allowed us to know that the Add, Remove and Contains methods of the ArrayList class work as expected. No failures or errors were encountered during the test. This indicates that the ArrayList class is stable and behaves correctly according to the requirements.
```
```
Testing is an integral part of the software testing process and it shows how well a piece of code is designed, implemented, and debugged. In this process, it's critical to test different scenarios, edge cases, possible failure points and validate outputs. By doing the said, we can be confident that our software behaves as expected in all situations. Test driven development is a software development process that can help developers to write testable software.
```
```
When it comes to testing a system, we usually break down the requirements into smaller units called system units. We can use automated testing tools and methodologies to test these units. In the scope of this article, we focused on testing the 'Add' and 'Remove' methods of the ArrayList class. But tests can be carried out on any component, system, or process according to the scope of the requirements or development of the software.
```
```
While automated testing is a very powerful tool, manual testing is equally important. Since automated testing can only execute a subset of tests, we need a way to test remaining parts manually. This is where manual testing comes in. Manual testing helps to validate parts of the system that automated tests cannot reach or can’t verify due to their complexities and nuances. This is especially useful when we have simple tests that automated tests can easily handle. Manual testing also provides a higher standard of confidence, because it can be done by someone who is familiar with the system and can validate the test results. Manual testing can also bring out real-time issues and can provide a better understanding of complexities.
```
```
While it is possible to conduct manual testing using paper and pencil, most of the modern software testing is done using specialized software tools and platforms. Tools like Selenium, JMeter, Postman, etc. This is an integrated set of functionalities that allows testers to perform different types of testing in a more efficient and automated way.
```
```
The primary objectives of manual testing are to verify and validate the functionality of the system under test(SUT) and to explore the boundary of the SUT. Manual testing can be used wherever automated testing is used. However, due to the wide scope and--------------------	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in old array 
        }
    }

}

//ArrayList.Test/ArrayList.Test/ArrayListStateTest.cs
﻿using System;
using System.Text;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions;

using NUnit.Framework;
using ArrayList;
using PexAPIWrapper;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture]
    public class ArrayListStateTest
    {
        #region State Test Cases
        [PexMethod]
        public void ADD_TestState([PexAssumeNone]ArrayList arrList, int x, 
                                 [PexAssertUnderTest]ArrayList resList, int expectedRet)
        {
            int ret = arrList.Add(x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList", arrList);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$resList", resList);
            Assert.AreEqual(expectedRet, ret);
        }

        [Test]
        [PexMethod]
        public void REMOVE_TestState([PexAssumeNone]ArrayList arrList, int x, 
                                    [PexAssertUnderTest]ArrayList resList)
        {
            arrList.Remove(x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList", arrList);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$resList", resList);
        }

        [Test]
        [PexMethod]
        public void INSERT_TestState([PexAssumeNone]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index, 
                                    [PexAssertUnderTest]ArrayList resList)
        {
            arrList.Insert(index, x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList", arrList);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$resList", resList);
        }

        [Test]
        [PexMethod]
        public void SET_TestState([PexAssumeNone]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index, 
                                 [PexAssertUnderTest]ArrayList resList)
        {
            arrList[index] = x;
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$arrList", arrList);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$resList", resList);
        }
        #endregion
    }
}

